# Check content with the linter

The Tech Docs Template includes a content linting task that uses [Vale](https://vale.sh/) and the [technical writing style guide rules](https://github.com/alphagov/tech-docs-linter). It checks your built HTML and Markdown for potential style problems and reports errors, warnings and suggestions.

Review each alert before changing your content. The linter does not make changes automatically or replace a content review.

## Set up the linter

Start with a documentation project that you can [build locally](../../create_project/build/). Run these commands from the project root, where your `Gemfile` and `config.rb` are located.

1. Install your project's dependencies with `bundle install`.
2. [Install Vale](https://vale.sh/docs/install) and check that `vale --version` works in your terminal.
3. Download the style rules using the configuration included in the installed gem:

   ```shell
   vale_config="$(bundle show govuk_tech_docs)/.vale.ini"
   vale --config="$vale_config" sync
   ```

The task uses the gem's `.vale.ini`, not a `.vale.ini` in your project. Run the sync command again after upgrading the gem. Downloading the rules requires network access and permission to write to the gem's `styles` directory.

To check that your version of the gem includes the task, run:

```shell
bundle exec tech_docs_jobs -T
```

Look for `lint:vale` in the output.

## Run a content check

```shell
bundle exec tech_docs_jobs lint:vale
```

By default, this removes the existing `./build` directory, runs `bundle exec middleman build`, then checks the HTML and Markdown files under `./build`. It prints a detailed report followed by a summary of errors, warnings and suggestions.

To check an existing build without rebuilding it:

```shell
bundle exec tech_docs_jobs 'lint:vale[./build,false,true]'
```

### Task options

Pass options in this order: `target`, `clean_build`, `full_output`. Quote the whole task argument so your shell does not interpret the square brackets.

| Option | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `target` | `./build` | Directory containing the HTML and Markdown files to check. |
| `clean_build` | `true` | Run the clean Middleman build first. Use `false` to check an existing build. |
| `full_output` | `true` | Print the detailed report. Use `false` to print only the summary. |

For example, to check an existing custom output directory and show only the summary:

```shell
bundle exec tech_docs_jobs 'lint:vale[./site-output,false,false]'
```

Changing `target` does not change Middleman's build directory. Build your custom output directory separately and set `clean_build` to `false`.

## Run in a pipeline

After your pipeline has installed Ruby, Bundler, the project's dependencies and Vale, add these shell commands:

```shell
vale_config="$(bundle show govuk_tech_docs)/.vale.ini"
vale --config="$vale_config" sync
bundle exec middleman build
bundle exec tech_docs_jobs 'lint:vale[./build,false,true]'
```

Keep the full output in your job logs so reviewers can see which files need attention.

The `lint:vale` task is advisory: reported content alerts do not make it fail. A successful exit is not proof that the content has no errors. A file that takes more than 10 seconds is skipped and reported as `HUNG (Skipped due to timeout)`, so check the logs for incomplete checks too. Build failures or unhandled exceptions can still fail the task.

## Troubleshoot checks

- If `vale` cannot be found, install it and make sure it is on the same `PATH` used by the terminal or CI job.
- If style rules are missing, repeat the sync command with the gem's configuration and check write permissions on its `styles` directory.
- If the task reports zero files, check that `target` contains a completed build with HTML or Markdown files.
- If a file is skipped after a timeout, investigate it before treating the report as complete. You can run Vale directly against that file with the same configuration to inspect its output.
